WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.580
Defender of the Crown, to survive this nightmare, your mind better be as sharp as your sword.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:29.880
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.420
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:32.800
And I'm Joe.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.260
Guys, Defender of the Crown, a game that is on the NES, but also just a version, you know,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.700
you could just play this game online right now on cinemaware.com or something like that.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.920
I'll put a link in the show notes, but I think it's just funny that like, this is just a game

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.500
now that is free, but people probably paid 60 bucks for it back then.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.620
I mean, it's not the NES version though, right?

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.740
No, I think it's probably the...

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.200
Like Amiga? Amiga was the, yeah, the version of this game that was very popular.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.320
You know, it's a very European game, right?

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.320
It takes place in England and all that.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.900
And I don't think like, I didn't feel any attachment to these characters being an American.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.460
That is such a weird thing to hop on.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:26.600
I mean, did you not like, you didn't like, you didn't like knights in shining armor, media?

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.040
Like that's all like, there's kids stuff like that.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.360
Mike was only a cowboy guy.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.300
Just, only cowboys.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.780
It's funny though that you bring that up because I didn't know where I was going with that,

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.680
but thank you, Sean.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:36.940
Like, you're actually right though.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:37.980
I hate all that stuff.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:38.980
Medieval times.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.960
You know, like all that stuff just not interesting to me.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.040
Jousting. Boring.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.360
Sorry to say it.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:52.680
You're telling me that if like ESPN ate the Ocho had like a jousting tournament, you wouldn't watch it?

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.440
I'd have to know the players.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.700
Okay. You know, like I couldn't just go in there.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.560
And I'm not saying they have to be celebrities.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.340
I'm just saying like, I'd have to be invested.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.260
I can't just go in there and pick a guy and say, oh, I hope he wins today.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.180
Because I'm going to be honest, even just playing jousting in this game was a little confusing.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.080
I'm not sure I'm going to be able to fully follow it on ESPN the Ocho.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.900
You didn't like A Knight's Tale starring Heath Ledger?

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:18.580
I never saw it.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.860
I did hear good things, but, you know, that's my own problem.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.840
So I'll add that to the watch list.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.720
Yeah. I'm just really hoping now that ESPN comes out with a celebrity jousting.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.480
I think, honestly, like NBC could probably do it.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.840
Yeah. That'd be great for Peacock.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.160
It would be a great, like, Olympic game.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.780
We've been doing a bunch of Olympic games.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.580
Right. Defender of the Crown, though, is not an Olympic game.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.640
It's not a series of minigames either.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.880
It's not just like you joust, you swing maces, all that stuff.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:03:02.340
This is another one of our, like, you know, grander, not grand, but, like, you know, a larger strategy-like game where it's England after the death of which king?

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:03.900
Does anybody remember who the king is?

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.800
One of those kings. One of those kings in, like, the 1100s, what have you.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.540
And all these different factions now are fighting for territorial control of England.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.120
And the only winner can be the one who conquers all the territories, which is what you'll do.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.460
You'll be one of those factions.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:30.260
You'll start with your own castle, conquer other territories and other castles, take on rival factions, and hopefully conquer all the territories so that you can become the new king of England.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:40.540
I think as a concept, even though I'm not attached to the Middle Ages, that's fairly straightforward of a game for, like, you know, a grand strategy-style game, right?

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.740
Like, the idea of just conquer all the territories, very, like, the board game Risk, you know?

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.280
It's easy to grapple.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.120
I understand what's going on.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:50.360
I understand my objective.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.960
Now it's just, okay, but how do I do that?

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.180
What did you guys think?

00:03:54.180 --> 00:04:02.080
Well, I think the first thing that I'm sure came up in everybody's mind was that, like, comparisons to Nobunaga's ambition, right?

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:08.420
Yeah. Like, it's the only game that we've really, like, played that was, like, kind of going for the same thing.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:22.740
And I think that maybe because we had just played that relatively recently, playing this makes me feel, I don't know, appreciative that it's, like, a little easier to understand.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:29.780
And I'm not really sure if I hadn't played Nobunaga's ambition if I would have been maybe a little more aggravated by the things that were harder to understand.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.240
But, like, by comparison, this is, like, oh, I get it.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.120
I know what I'm doing.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.320
Like, you know, there might be some times where I'm a little confused.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:46.900
But, like, as you figure it out, not that Nobunaga's ambition is not a functioning game, but it takes a lot of work on your part to, like, understand how it functions.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:55.620
Whereas this, I feel like you can pretty quickly get into the swing of it and, like, fully understand it and, like, know what your objectives are and know how to go about it.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.020
Know what you're doing right. Know what you're doing wrong.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.820
See, I sort of had a different experience.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:02.680
I couldn't really.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.840
I was interested in the.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:07.060
I'm interested in these kinds of games.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.940
I'm interested in this game.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:23.660
And as much as I didn't really understand Nobunaga's ambition, I understood, like, the basics, but, you know, couldn't game the system in the way that a veteran would be able to.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:26.520
I think I understood that more than I understood this one.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:31.060
And I don't think it has anything to do with, like, the complexity of the game because it is.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.200
It isn't as complex as Nobunaga's ambition.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:51.060
I think it had more to do with, like, I found that maybe the artificial intelligence in this game is just a little different than what I was expecting in terms of, like, what your enemies are doing.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:06:00.500
And there was just some clarity that was missing for me as to, like, what the state of my opponents was.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.580
And, like, we'll get into it.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:09.860
But I think I had some issues with this one while still appreciating a lot about it.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:24.480
Well, Sean, you might remember in our Nobunaga's ambition episode, which was just you and me, Joe gave his thoughts the following episode, that we were talking about did they need, like, all of these different stats and occurrences to happen?

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.460
Like, did they give you too much freedom in control?

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.700
And you can't really make, like, a verdict there.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:37.260
You can have an opinion for sure, but it's impossible to say because the developers obviously wanted you to be in control of all those things.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:47.500
In this particular game, Defender of the Crown, you're really just playing as the actual leader of your faction in an army-like setting.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.780
You don't have to worry about your towns.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:56.640
You don't have to worry about, you know, taxing your citizens or the happiness of your citizens.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.860
You're just building an army, you know, conquering territory and taking on other factions.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:15.100
So that's, like, for me, the most apparent difference between this and Nobunaga's ambition is that, like, the goal of Nobunaga's was too open-ended for me.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:23.020
And here I got the idea of just conquer territories early and often and build your army as fast as possible.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.620
And you were talking about the AI.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:33.180
And something I noticed is with the AI in Nobunaga's, you saw what they were doing, you know?

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.720
Like, you were able to – you could even watch it all.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.020
Yeah. Which is crazy.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:45.800
In this game, it's happening and you kind of have the ability to see them take territories via the map-changing colors and stuff like that.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:51.900
But you don't really know what's going on until you take it to your turn and start evaluating what they've done.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.280
And I have no way to, like, confirm this or not.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.220
But it seems like the AI is very busy during their turns.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:03.100
And there's never really, like, a good moment to strike them.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.140
They're always about, at the very least, even with you, at least stronger.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:15.580
Yeah, going back to your first point about, like, sort of the main difference between these two strategy games that we played pretty close to each other now,

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:22.800
is that I think I preferred the focus, even though, again, like, I had trouble understanding both of them.

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:25.660
Because I didn't play enough of it.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:29.860
I think if I played 40 hours of each, I'd have a better comparison.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:37.200
But at least, like, at its core, what I appreciated about Nobunaga's Ambition is that while it's not, like, a nation-building game,

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.620
it is still, like, it's more administration-focused.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:52.380
Like, there's more scope to it than Defender of the Crown, which is more, yes, expansion, like in Nobunaga's Ambition,

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:58.880
but also focusing on, like, the gallantry thing with all the weird minigames in here.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:13.840
But I think, like, when it comes down to, yeah, the AI, there was, yeah, it was just that I couldn't, I could not tell what was going on.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:17.200
I was playing the game wrong the first two times I played it.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.220
I did the exact opposite of what you said.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:25.220
I basically just tried to amass an army first and then go on the attack.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:28.100
And by then, like, I was the last blue standing.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:33.020
So, yeah, I'm rambling now, but this is my experience.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:40.580
Yeah, exactly. And I think I have, like, a big concept thing that I want to save for later, but I'm going to save it for later for sure.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:44.700
But I want to say that it's going to answer the kind of thing you were just mentioning,

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:53.080
because I do think it's important to understand that the developers of this game did set up the game in a particular way.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:54.420
Joe, you were going to add something?

00:09:54.420 --> 00:10:10.120
Yeah, well, I was just going to ask, so not to harp on this too long, but so as far as the AI, the enemy AI, like, what about it was, what element of it did you find, like, was making it more challenging for, you know, to understand what was happening?

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:24.540
So, I think there was a point, because, again, this is in, this is while I was playing the game wrong, which is, I mean, I pretty much just played the game wrong the whole time, where I was sort of stuck.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:29.680
And I just kept getting attacked in my home province.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:45.780
And no matter how many times I fought them off in the, like, archery minigame, and then I would, like, have some gold and I'd buy some replacements to refill my garrison, that next turn, it would happen again.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:11:02.540
And so I was stuck in this, like, negative, positive, whatever, feedback loop of just getting attacked over and over, defending for as many times as I could, and after each attack, just buying just enough to be able to defend myself and not, like, grow.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:07.740
So, after however many repetitions of this, I was just frustrated.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:12.860
And I know that this is my fault, but that was my experience with the game.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.140
Okay, I hear that now.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:20.140
Yeah, I wonder if that is, like, just something that you can get yourself stuck into.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:30.400
Yeah, because my thing was, like, oh, are they, like, do they have sources of income that I don't as a player to, like, sort of buff the computer?

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:37.660
What did you use, what was your most, what was your source of income mostly, just, like, the, like...

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:47.980
I mean, I did some raiding when I wasn't getting attacked in the earlier game, and then I was just sort of, like, living off of the base amount.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:56.720
Yeah. Because I feel like, yeah, I think it just played out differently for me, but I did, first of all, I did a lot of raiding.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.640
Raiding was, like, my second income.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:02.500
Like, whenever I didn't want to attack, I would just raid a castle.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:08.060
Because I also found that that was, like, the easiest of the minigames for me to, like, get good at.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:14.800
Yeah. So I would do that minigame, I would come back with money, I would buy soldiers, and I would put them in different places.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.480
And then I would be able to get more castles and get more territories.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.340
So then each turn I would get more money.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:24.260
And I spent a couple turns, like, just building that way, getting a couple territories, like, letting my money build.

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:32.000
And then went on to, like, finally start attacking when I had, like, a good amount of money, a good amount of soldiers, a good amount of knights.

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:41.180
But, yeah, so, I mean, I didn't feel, at least at first, like I was getting, like, kind of screwed by the AI.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:47.460
There was a point later on where I felt like I was screwed by it, but I do feel like I could have, you know, that was just me losing the game later in the game.

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.200
Yeah, I find that that's me.

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:56.560
That's what was happening. Yeah, I don't want to get too much further into, like, the tactics and strategy because I don't feel like we've accurately described the game enough yet, breaking it down into pieces.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:03.280
But I was just going to mention, Joe, if I made it seem like the AI is cheating or too difficult, that wasn't my intention.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:10.980
My intention was that Nobunaga's ambition requires more possibilities and more options to every single.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:17.680
Right, more variables. So the AI could just wildly vary from your strategy, but you might be leading them in another department.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:33.900
In this particular game, you know, for better or for worse, the AI is basically just, you know, working on the same stuff, taking territories that you can't possibly get to yet and building up an army that's, at the very least, going to match your numbers, if not surprise you with how many numbers they have.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:39.120
Now, you can always check the status of your AI, but only on your particular turn.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:40.740
It doesn't cost you a turn or anything.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:42.120
You can always check in on everybody.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:49.420
But I think it's just surprising to see how strong their armies grow and how fast they conquer that land.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:57.300
And, you know, that's probably the intention of the game is, like, if we break this down for a minute, right, you have a selection of characters.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:02.060
And those characters, other than just having different names and portraits, the biggest thing is their attributes.

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:05.080
You have leadership, jousting, and sword play.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:13.860
And for each of the different characters that you choose, they either have a weak, an average, a strong, and a good.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:15.540
So strong being the best.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.140
I did them out of order there for a second.

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:20.960
But it varies between them.

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:29.240
Personally, you know, I didn't think about the character selection too much, although I suppose that is some of the fun of the game.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:36.480
But Cedric of Rotherwood has, at the very least, just average stats with a strong stat as well.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.560
He's not weak at any particular thing.

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.920
And so I just gravitated to him.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:42.920
I mean, it's nice to have a choice.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:54.920
But with the learning curve of the game, you know, you're best off probably just starting with the most average player and figuring out the mechanics that way than giving yourself a handicap.

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:57.600
Yeah, I actually also went with Cedric.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:04.660
But for different reasons, I just went with Cedric because the leadership was strong, which I knew would help in, like, overall battles.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:09.800
And then the one that was worse was jousting, which I was like, well, I probably won't do that much jousting anyways.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:11.100
And I didn't, actually.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:18.380
But I think if you're good at jousting, that means that you can very easily, like, raise your leadership.

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.980
But that's another section.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:30.100
Yeah. But then I also wasn't sure if, like, if it was just better for me to just start with strong leadership and then not have to worry about raising it.

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:31.820
I don't know if it gets lowered at any point.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:33.860
I don't know. In which case you'd have to raise it again.

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:37.940
But, you know, just as a rookie, I was just like, oh, I'll go with Cedric also.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:41.460
It is weird how they weight the attributes, though, right?

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:51.280
Like, leadership is very important because that's how your army, the whole thing you're building throughout the game, that's how well they respond to your commands.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.760
So you would want that to be strong.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:16:04.180
Jousting is just something that can either help you claim certain land against other people or gain the leadership category anyway.

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:08.580
So you might as well, again, start with someone with strong leadership rather than build it up via jousting.

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:11.260
Although I suppose you could conquer some territories that way.

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:13.720
Can't conquer castles through jousting, though.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:21.220
And then sword play, I actually thought about, like, switching to somebody who was strong in sword play because I was struggling with that for a minute.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:25.620
But it only increases the health bar of your sword character.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:29.100
It doesn't make them better at striking or anything like that.

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.420
That's still entirely up to you and how you do it.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:37.320
So it's weird that jousting and sword play become almost these, as Sean was describing, the minigame stats.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.140
But leadership is the one that actually matters.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:52.920
Yeah. From an army perspective, you know, they should have had maybe more, you know, like a wisdom attribute or an intelligence attribute, you know, that mattered to other things involving your army.

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:55.820
Or like tactics or something.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.360
Yeah. But I guess that is sort of covered by leadership.

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:15.960
But, yeah, I've read about or I watched a little bit of other critique of the game and heard that you can basically beat this game without really leaving the menu aside from, like, the times that you actually need to.

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.460
Like, you don't have to do any raids.

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:23.700
You don't have to do any defense minigames.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:31.200
It's like if you've really mastered the game, you don't have to do any of the other things that these stats are controlling.

00:17:32.140 --> 00:17:33.040
Yeah. I found that.

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:34.820
I saw that, too.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:36.300
I don't know how you do that.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.620
But I saw that that was a way of going about it, too.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:46.920
And I think, like, I'm assuming that, like, anyone who plays it kind of has their own, like, variation of, like, how much of that they do.

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:49.400
Like, for me, like, I never held a tournament.

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.920
You know, I think I was, like, invited to one tournament.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:54.180
But otherwise, I was like, I don't want to do this.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:18:01.480
I don't care about, you know, right now I'm not trying to get my, what is it, like, you get more respect, which ups your leadership if you hold tournaments.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:03.880
And you can make money if you hold tournaments.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.380
But I was just like, I don't feel like doing the jousting minigame.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:08.360
I was like, I never did that unless I needed to.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:12.120
And, like, the, you know, like I said, I did a lot of raids.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:18.360
So that's when you do the raids, you end up doing, like, a little sword fight minigame, which I did a lot of.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:25.760
And then the other one that came up a lot for me was when my castle, one of my castles is being attacked.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:30.040
And you got to fight off the people with the crossbones, which I have mixed feelings about that minigame.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:36.180
I hate it. But I don't know if you want me to jump into that now if we're going to talk about the minigames.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:42.120
Well, you know, I did, just before we get to the minigames, Joe, I wanted to just talk about the map itself.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.980
You know, it's just, it's clearly the map of England.

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:51.760
But the way that the territories are divided and where you start are kind of important to the overall game.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:57.540
Because you can only ever be a, I guess, a northern lord in this game, right?

00:18:57.580 --> 00:18:58.940
You can only, you only ever start in the north.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:02.200
You only have, like, three choices of possible castles to start at.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:10.460
One of the castles is far superior than the other ones, which, you know, just a strategy that I read on GameFAQs.

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:11.660
Because you got Robin Hood, right?

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.780
What's that? Because you got Robin Hood, like, right there?

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:17.700
It's actually, it gives you more gold.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:19.540
Oh. Yeah.

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:22.280
The territory starts you with more gold.

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:24.380
It starts with 11 instead of 10.

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:26.800
And then you gain an extra gold bonus.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:28.340
So you just get soldiers quicker.

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:30.220
I forget which one it is.

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:33.920
I think it's the most centered of the northern castles.

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:38.140
But maybe they give you that because if you're the most centered, you're, well, I guess you're surrounded by allies.

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:40.260
So it's not. Semi-allies.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:41.600
You're more exposed. So, yeah.

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:50.240
But you're more exposed. So, like, if more places get taken over, you're, like, I liked, you know, when you, for Cedric, you know, you're right on, like, the coast.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:54.280
So you, there's no one, there's no one north of you that can attack.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:57.180
So you're, like, safe as long as you defend to the south.

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:02.340
Yeah. So, like, I wonder if that's why they give you that extra gold.

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:07.140
It, like, can give you that advantage in the center castle because you're exposed on all sides.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:09.860
That sounds like a good balance thing.

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:20.740
Yeah. You're exposed on all sides, you know, and I think that's the interesting thing here in general, right, is that there's a lot of open territory, most of it in the south.

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:32.280
But the game moves very quickly from the sense of all this open territory to everybody just kind of quickly claiming the open land and then figuring out where to go from there.

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:39.200
Do they take the land from others with the expectation that the other armies won't be there so it's, like, a free capture?

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:47.580
Do they go for the castles to take down the, you know, the rival faction before they can accumulate too much land?

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:56.660
The land matters so much in this game, and it's interesting that the unoccupied spaces have bonuses of their own for the person who conquers them first.

00:20:56.660 --> 00:21:02.480
You know, like, you get a gold bonus and a vassal bonus provided to the first person.

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:05.000
You can't just take it from somebody else and acquire that bonus.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:10.060
But, you know, you have this ability to move freely within your own land too.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:24.080
So there's a lot of things that the map offers to you that I appreciate it almost that even though it seems like the land could be conquered very quickly, it matters right from the very beginning which direction you choose to go in.

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:31.020
You could create a, you know, in Risk there's always that strategy of, like, starting in Australia and creating the walled garden.

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:42.280
You know, you could try to do that from the northernmost point and just kind of continue to work your way in so that they have to fight you in order to get to your more precious things like your castle.

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:55.520
But you could also take the stance that, like, no, it's better to take these guys, to take these middle spaces earlier to challenge the, you know, the bonuses and the land that you're going up against with your enemies.

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:58.060
Any other thoughts on the map from you guys?

00:21:58.060 --> 00:22:03.800
I guess one thing, this isn't, like, specific to this game.

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:14.460
It's more just a comparison to other strategy games that deal with maps is that I was sort of surprised that there wasn't really much of, like, a terrain bonus thing.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:20.100
I guess, like, England, it's not a very varied landscape.

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:22.840
So it wouldn't really apply too much.

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:33.980
But when it comes to territory expansion in strategy games, like, I'm always looking for choke points and there just isn't any in this map.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:43.120
Which I guess, like, sort of takes away from some strategic thinking but also makes it so you can't just rely on them.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:45.400
That's a fair point, you know.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:48.000
There's not a lot of personality on this map either.

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:54.100
Like, I think the only locations that have names would be the ones with the castles, correct?

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:57.500
I don't quite remember.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:00.100
I don't think you can name check the territories otherwise.

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:03.420
Yeah. I know that the castle ones definitely do.

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:09.860
But, I mean, I've played too much Crusader Kings not to, like, associate them with others.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:24.840
So now that we have a layout of the objective of the game and the way that your leadership stats work and the map itself,

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:28.060
what's the first thing you do in a game like this?

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:29.820
You have a lot of different options, right?

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:34.080
You can build up your army by spending your gold to buy an army.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:37.160
You can raid a castle to get some money.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:38.840
You could host a tournament.

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:43.660
You know, there's a lot of – you could conquer territories that haven't been claimed yet.

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:45.380
Joe, what did you do first?

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:49.880
Well, I mean, if we want to talk about what I really, really did first was like, oh, what is this game?

00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:52.780
Let me attack some people, lose really fast, and then start over.

00:23:53.120 --> 00:24:07.540
But then when I did first, once I already sort of had an idea of what I was doing was, you know, my strategy, like I kind of mentioned earlier, was to start by, you know, I would defend my castle, you know, like, as much as I can, buy what I could.

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:14.600
And then for a few turns, just raid other castles and start building up money so I could build up my army.

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:27.000
So then I could – once I had, like, a decent army, a couple of knights, you know, a couple of catapults and a good number of soldiers, I would start taking over, like, a territory or two around me.

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:43.180
Then raid more castles, get more money, take a few more turns to get the income from now these other territories, and then kind of, like, do that again but, like, exponentially bigger because now I have more money, I can buy more things and try and slowly start, like, working my way out.

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:46.800
And that was really kind of, like, my gameplay loop.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:54.440
And obviously, like, a lot of those turns you have to spend – especially when you have more castles and more territories, you have to spend defending your territories.

00:24:55.180 --> 00:25:05.320
And I'd be interested in seeing how, like, an expert does this without doing any of the minigames because that was, like, my biggest hindrance was, like, okay, I want to do this, but now I got to do this minigame.

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:11.380
And, like, it all comes down to my skill at it, which at first was definitely a big setback.

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:12.920
I did get better at a couple of them.

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:16.740
Like I said, I was good at the raids.

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:23.200
And I also got better at when – at the minigame that occurs when people attack your castle.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:24.700
So, like, I don't know.

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:26.000
It was a varied thing.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:46.640
But I think I actually sort of in my playthrough sort of neglected a good chunk of this game that I'd be interested in going back and playing, like, maybe with more focus on, you know, on tournaments or some of the other, like, things that you can do to build up your army.

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:47.540
Sorry, I'm kind of rambling.

00:25:47.720 --> 00:25:54.220
But there's just a lot of – there's a lot of paths you can take, it seems like, to move forward.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:26:10.860
The first thing I did sort of completely counter to what Joe did was host a tournament because I'm like, all right, I mean, I know that this is going to be mostly just conquering territory.

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:14.580
So, let's see what makes this game different.

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:21.880
So, I did that and immediately sucked at jousting and then kind of got the hang of swinging the chain.

00:26:23.840 --> 00:26:26.300
And what was the third thing you did?

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:27.340
I don't quite remember.

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:29.640
But – Raiding the castles with the –

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:30.680
No, tournament.

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:33.440
Oh, tournament. No, isn't that it?

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:37.500
I think it's just the jousting and the macing.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:39.220
Okay. I thought there was a third thing.

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:50.620
But anyway, so I did that and I'm like, oh, okay, I can, like, sort of use this as a way to, like, get some soft power.

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:59.240
But then I realized that, like, yeah, you're just sort of raising a stat and you're not really gaining much by hosting this tournament aside from, like, a little bit of gold.

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:01.180
So, I was like, okay, I'm not going to do that again.

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:02.940
I'll just go to the ones that I'm invited.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:10.960
And then that's where I sort of, like, lost my way and made bad decisions throughout.

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:17.080
So, yeah. But I did try and just do the tournament thing and see where that took me.

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:27.420
Yeah. I'd argue the most important thing for me to do before thinking about even hosting a tournament or taking on a rival faction was just building my own army.

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:33.160
You know, you start with 20 soldiers in your castle, the space that you occupy.

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:38.180
And, you know, depending on where I was on the map, I just kind of said, screw it.

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:43.300
Like, you know, when you buy people for your army, they always go back to your castle anyway.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:49.480
So, I took all those guys out of my castle, put them right into my garrison and just said, like, let's ride, boys.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:50.760
Like, we got 20 of us.

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:51.840
Like, let's go.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:52.920
Let's take some territories.

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:58.380
And, you know, I would immediately go for the territories too because you want that gold bonus.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:09.860
You want to be able to buy more soldiers right away too and eventually the catapult because the catapult is the only way that you can take on the castles and take them over to begin with.

00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:18.420
But if we could just talk about the strategy of buying in general, it's weighted in the sense where soldiers are just one gold.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:27.140
So, theoretically, you know, soldiers are something that should be plentiful to you at any particular time, especially as the game goes on and you acquire a lot of gold.

00:28:27.740 --> 00:28:33.460
You could buy – you could have a soldier army of, like, you know, 40, 50, 60 people.

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:36.200
You know, it ate caps at 250 per unit.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:41.860
So, clearly the game was expecting people to have high army counts if games went on for a lot longer.

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:43.740
Knights are 8 gold.

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:45.280
Catapults are 15 gold.

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:52.760
And the castles thing, which I didn't fully understand, I think that's 25 or 20.

00:28:52.760 --> 00:28:53.880
I can't remember exactly.

00:28:54.200 --> 00:29:00.700
But for me, you know, this was mostly a game of buying a ton of soldiers, maybe a few knights and stuff like that.

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:11.900
But the idea that buying these guys sends them back to your home base, not where you are, it could work in your favor if you're constantly taking guys out of there anyway.

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:14.340
So, this way you're replenishing your stock without being there.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:20.340
And since you can travel freely amongst your own territories, it's not like it's going to cost you a turn to go back.

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:24.020
Hopefully, it's not going to cost you a turn to go back to your castle.

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:31.700
I just thought it was a little strange that the idea of buying your army, but you're not buying them at the place where you are.

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:40.200
You know, I could imagine that you'd be on the territory paying a different sum or whatever depending on whether you own the territory or have a castle there or something.

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:42.040
The game didn't really do that.

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:48.400
Instead, it's flat rates throughout the entire game, something that could easily be taken advantage of as you get closer to the late game.

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:49.520
That is true.

00:29:50.460 --> 00:30:00.540
So, I think a lot of where you see that as odd comes from, like, you sort of started out by saying that you are playing as your guy.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:07.980
And always, like, as your guy leading the army, wherever he may go, like, that's your perspective of where you are.

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:22.180
Whereas, I guess, like, when this sort of crosses into, like, a real-time strategy thing, I know this isn't that, but when I think of playing, like, StarCraft or something, like, when I make infantry, they come out of my base.

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:32.320
And so, I think that, like, if you also apply that to, like, well, if you're recruiting soldiers, you're, like, you're raising your levy in your home province.

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:34.820
So, that sort of made a bit of sense to me.

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:40.940
But I kind of want to touch on what you said at the end there, too, which is where everything's a flat rate.

00:30:42.440 --> 00:30:49.620
And that does, like, make it so that, like, in the late game, having a bunch of guys is almost, like, a given.

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:55.020
So, it sort of makes it so that, like, this game lives and dies.

00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:58.020
Your playthrough lives and dies by the early game.

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:17.100
And if you've made some mistakes and your gold to guys economy isn't up to par, the fact that it costs the same to you as it does, like, the biggest opponent, there are no, like, size malices, you could easily just get steamrolled.

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:24.200
And it may take several turns for you to actually lose, but you will lose.

00:31:24.980 --> 00:31:26.940
Yeah, and I get that.

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:37.060
And maybe this is kind of artificially created, but I do, I don't hate the idea that, like, that you live and die by your early game.

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:45.120
Because then it feels like when you do well, you know, you play and you do okay and maybe, you know, you're like me and you lose, like, halfway through.

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:57.400
But now I feel like if I can figure out that early game and I can get to that point, like, even if it's artificially created, I feel like it would feel so rewarding to finally be like, wow, like, now I'm, like, unstoppable.

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:01.240
Because I found the right path to get here.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:05.460
Oh, yeah. It's very, it's a great power trip once you've done it.

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:15.660
But I think it sort of takes away from how dynamic a game can feel if, like, there's no, not to call it, like, a rubber band thing, like, in a racing game.

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:24.260
But that there isn't different play styles that, like, oh, if you play like this, like, you're going to have, it's going to be difficult early.

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:35.360
But later you're going to start to really start to dominate or, like, oh, this is easy because in the beginning you'll have an advantage.

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.200
But later on it becomes a little more cumbersome.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:42.840
Like, I guess it would need to be a much more complex game to have that sort of feel.

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:48.340
But I guess I've been using most of my time saying, like, this should be like other games.

00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:50.680
No, but I see what you're saying.

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:52.260
And I don't disagree with you.

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:57.860
I wasn't even saying, like, just for the power trip element of it.

00:32:57.920 --> 00:33:03.240
But I mean for, like, the journey to get to that power level, like, it does feel satisfying.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:06.840
But I think that's, like, a different point than arguing with what you're saying.

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:08.120
I agree with what you're saying, too.

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:17.640
Yeah. You know, I like that it gives you that, you know, way to, like, find your path to getting that powerful.

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:28.520
But I also, you know, I see that, like, there could definitely be some more complexity to some of these systems that lets you, like...

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:30.120
There isn't just one way to win.

00:33:30.680 --> 00:33:32.420
Right, right. Yeah.

00:33:32.580 --> 00:33:38.880
I mean, it's all the, you know, that early game being so important is all the more reason to make sure that your army is well-equipped from the very beginning.

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:51.480
I would say that, you know, I played a few playthroughs of this game, and the winning strategy for me was, you know, withdrawing my entire army from my castle and putting it directly into my garrison.

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:56.440
And then raiding other people's castles to get more gold right away.

00:33:56.440 --> 00:34:10.920
The only objective of the raid is just to be good at the sword play minigame, which is really just a funny side-scrolling, like, strike-with-pixel knives thing.

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:18.800
That, like, it's not the most intuitive thing, but it's also not that hard to learn how to be good at it if, you know, your game depends on it.

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:25.140
Yeah. Like, if that's one of the major things, like, sorry, but you must learn that mechanic or you're not going to have a good time with this game.

00:34:25.140 --> 00:34:30.640
And once you do learn, and, you know, I'm not saying I found a cheat or anything.

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:52.740
I just think I found a way to do this and a way to consistently win at this sword play game that it made it easier to have a big army at the start of the game, which is a nice feeling, sure, but also makes it so this way it kind of diminishes other strategies for playing this game when I know that this works better.

00:34:53.180 --> 00:35:05.380
Yeah. So let's talk about the raiding of the castle since that was the most recent thing with the swords play and everything like that.

00:35:05.720 --> 00:35:11.640
Again, the sword play stat of your character is just dependent on how much health that they get.

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:22.160
You always attack three small health enemies on the outside of the castle and then someone on the inside who has a much larger, significantly larger health bar.

00:35:22.160 --> 00:35:28.260
This is a side scrolling, which is weird to say, right, because it's not really scrolling.

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:38.240
So it's just a 2D swordsman's game, much like the fencing game that we played in the track and field two.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:41.040
It's a fencing beat them up with one enemy per screen.

00:35:41.480 --> 00:35:42.860
With one enemy per screen.

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:48.100
I love it. Or, sorry, one enemy per time, but the screen doesn't change for the first three guys.

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:50.120
They just keep coming outside one by one.

00:35:50.460 --> 00:35:52.940
Why they wouldn't just come out all three at once?

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:55.160
Are they like, okay, he's not that serious.

00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:56.040
You go first.

00:35:56.120 --> 00:35:57.240
And then they're like, oh, shit, he's dead.

00:35:57.240 --> 00:35:58.040
I got to get outside.

00:35:58.480 --> 00:35:59.340
It's just that other warlord?

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:01.420
He didn't come with anybody else?

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:07.060
Yeah. He really should be sending out more of his army, especially when you later fight him and find out he has 60 soldiers.

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:08.820
You're like, why did I just take down four?

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:20.000
Anyway, this is a game idea here where you either strike high or strike low or defend.

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:27.560
I think the main objective for me here was just to gain as much control of the space as possible.

00:36:27.740 --> 00:36:29.520
And by that, I mean move forward.

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:33.680
Don't be backed into a corner because they walk slowly.

00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:37.920
You walk slowly. But you have enough time to continually gain ground.

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:47.720
And then it's as simple as a Mike Tyson's punch-out rhythm game thing where I would take a strike, take a step back.

00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:49.820
Take a strike, take a step back.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:52.400
You just strike, then step, strike, then step.

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:54.480
You'll eventually be in a corner, sure.

00:36:54.820 --> 00:36:57.760
But if you find yourself there, it's only because you didn't gain enough ground.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:06.640
Joe, you have some thoughts? No, I was going to say at first I thought that that was interesting because it sounded like what you were saying was the exact opposite of what I did, which I also thought I found a way.

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:10.760
But actually when you continued explaining it, I think you did the exact same thing.

00:37:10.880 --> 00:37:18.000
Yeah, gain ground at the beginning and then spend basically most of the actual fight sort of like half retreating while you're kind of stabbing at him.

00:37:18.080 --> 00:37:19.700
And it all just came down to timing.

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:23.180
And yeah, I did like the exact same thing.

00:37:23.300 --> 00:37:33.920
But I will say that I felt like as much as that gave me like the confidence that I knew if I did – if I was in the sword fight, I would probably win 75% of the time.

00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:39.280
There was still that 25% where I'm like – it still wasn't like a perfect plan, at least for me.

00:37:39.280 --> 00:37:41.700
Maybe I could have gotten better at it if I did it more.

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:42.600
So it wasn't a perfect plan.

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:46.040
And it still felt like there was a little bit of risk to raiding a castle.

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:47.060
Like I could lose.

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:49.200
Right, right. I mean, yeah.

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:51.000
I don't want to make it sound like I was perfect.

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:59.300
I guess it was like the idea of raiding for money being the most consistent way to win the game.

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:07.020
Almost like since I was taking the same steps every time, if you don't win the raid, you just reset because it's like it's not worth it.

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:11.020
You know, it's better to just follow the same steps again and try the raid.

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:13.800
Sean, any thoughts on the sword play?

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:14.800
Anything they could have done?

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:16.600
I mean, obviously they could have done a lot, right?

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:27.360
The striking mechanic even, just the ability to only either strike or play defense is better in some regards, right?

00:38:27.460 --> 00:38:28.400
But it's not the full pick.

00:38:28.420 --> 00:38:36.740
Yeah, I mean, basically the only other thing that I can say differently from you guys is really just like whenever I was in that minigame,

00:38:36.740 --> 00:38:40.620
I just wanted to play Nidhogg instead.

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:42.560
Well, I mean, I always want to play Nidhogg.

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:45.640
Yeah. Sean, what is Nidhogg for the uninformed?

00:38:46.020 --> 00:38:53.800
Nidhogg is a fencing game where you try to gain ground on your opponent and eventually get eaten by a worm.

00:38:55.220 --> 00:39:01.440
And we'll put a link in the show notes for those of you that still don't understand, but it is a great game and we've all played it together.

00:39:01.640 --> 00:39:05.700
We have. Actually, I want to back up for a second.

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:12.940
Mike, you said that the best bet would be to reset if you're losing and just kind of try it again over and over again, right?

00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:15.160
That was sort of my point, too.

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:17.020
I did say that.

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:19.520
I think I know what you're about to say.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:24.220
Okay. I'm not suggesting that this is the one true way to play the game.

00:39:24.380 --> 00:39:40.020
It's just that through trial and effort, and I did eventually beat this game, I think your best bet is just to stick with what you know, stick with what's working, and just continually try based on that.

00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:42.380
I didn't want to ruin a good thing.

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:54.240
If I was doing well with this particular strategy, I didn't want to say, oh, well, maybe it's time to try tournaments or maybe it's time to go for more empty land early on and just have more spaces.

00:39:54.540 --> 00:39:55.680
Okay. Which is certainly a tactic.

00:39:55.980 --> 00:40:03.440
I would say that what Mike said is it's a little bit more extreme of what I was saying.

00:40:03.440 --> 00:40:09.140
Just like, oh, if you mess up in the early game, you're already screwed, so you may as well start over.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:14.140
But I think that after one failed raid, maybe that hasn't happened yet.

00:40:14.620 --> 00:40:15.500
Well, right, right.

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:22.140
And I get it for the early game if you want to start the game over, but just to be clear, you mean actually literally reset, like start over.

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:23.400
Oh, yeah, completely.

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:28.300
Yeah. So that's where I feel like you're doing yourself a disservice.

00:40:29.720 --> 00:40:41.380
Like, I feel like that takes away the enjoyment of the game because you're taking away the stakes of, like, well, I lost and now I have to deal with that and, like, learn how to –

00:40:41.380 --> 00:40:47.940
Like, I feel like that's the point of this game is to, like, you lost, you lose men, you lose time, you lose a turn, whatever.

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:50.080
Now you have to, like, maneuver around that.

00:40:50.180 --> 00:40:54.320
And, like, that's where I think the strategy comes in is, like, recovering when you lose.

00:40:54.320 --> 00:40:59.940
Well, that's sort of like – my point is that there isn't – there aren't a lot of opportunities to recover.

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:09.760
Like, have you won – did you win a game after you had lost, like, a couple times and, like, halfway through what you thought was halfway through the game?

00:41:10.420 --> 00:41:13.280
I mean, no, I didn't do that.

00:41:13.340 --> 00:41:19.120
But I also don't think that, like, a couple times when I failed what I was trying to do that that, like, sunk my game.

00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:34.660
Okay. So what I feel like, you know, just a little sidebar, like, what I feel like sunk my game could be something with what you were talking about earlier where I just suddenly started to feel like the enemies had gained so much – such huge armies, like, halfway through the game.

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:46.540
But I also, you know, I at the time had attributed that to, like, okay, maybe I started spreading myself too thin too early and I started trying to take too many territories and, you know, lost ground that way.

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:50.420
But, like, I never thought, like – I failed at a lot of things.

00:41:50.480 --> 00:41:55.700
You know, I would fail when I would go in to do a raid or I would fail, like, defending my castle.

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:58.440
But, like, you know, there was some fluctuation.

00:41:58.440 --> 00:42:01.460
Like, I would start to lose a little bit.

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:02.760
I would kind of come back a little bit.

00:42:02.880 --> 00:42:07.080
And then, you know, at one point, obviously, it just skyrocketed how much I was losing.

00:42:07.240 --> 00:42:08.780
But, like, I don't know.

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:10.840
I'd have to play it a few more times to, like –

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:15.320
Yeah, you're right. To, like, know if it is, like, possible or not.

00:42:15.320 --> 00:42:17.920
But I didn't feel like it was ever impossible while I was playing.

00:42:18.520 --> 00:42:19.800
I'm sure it's possible.

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:21.760
I'm sure there are people who are better at this game.

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:27.500
I'm just saying anecdotally that my one playthrough where I won, I was perfect.

00:42:27.500 --> 00:42:32.780
In the sense that not that nobody ever died or anything, but, like, that I never failed anything I set out to do.

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:36.100
If I was raiding a castle, I raided it successfully.

00:42:36.100 --> 00:42:46.120
If I was trying to take on an enemy castle, I catapulted, I threw the rocks successfully, knocked down the wall, and then, like, fought them head on.

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:47.720
And it all worked out for me.

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:53.540
You know, I don't know for certain that it can or can't be beat.

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:56.700
All I'm going to say to you, and this is, you know, just an idea.

00:42:56.880 --> 00:42:58.320
I don't need a response or anything like that.

00:42:58.460 --> 00:42:59.960
But you're suggesting –

00:43:00.500 --> 00:43:07.700
You're suggesting that it wasn't the failure that caused the enemies to gain ground on you.

00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:10.440
And I would think that that's what it would have to have been, right?

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:28.260
Is that, like, in a game where every single turn counts towards the, you know, the abilities of your army and everything, that in that particular failure or failures, that's actually where the enemy was gaining ground on you in terms of soldier count or land or gold?

00:43:28.860 --> 00:43:30.040
Well, sure. Of course.

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:31.580
Yeah. I mean, like any game.

00:43:31.920 --> 00:43:34.260
But then, you know, sometimes I would win it back.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:40.420
Yeah, but this one happens because now it's too late. Yeah, but not – but, you know, I don't think it was – my point is I don't think – and, again, like, I also don't know because I only know.

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:43.180
I only had, like, the experience where – you know, the one experience.

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:48.140
But I also think that, you know, like, it was – at one point that happened, yes.

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:50.660
But I feel like at several other points I would lose.

00:43:50.800 --> 00:43:51.780
But then I would gain it back.

00:43:51.880 --> 00:44:00.200
And I would – you know, I felt like – and maybe, yeah, the times I lost in the beginning somehow eventually contributed and was the cause of that.

00:44:00.340 --> 00:44:06.020
But my point is that I just don't think that – you know, I mean, that's how you win or lose any game.

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:17.020
Like, I don't think that it was so – at least it wasn't apparent to me that it was so unbalanced once you lose that, like, hey, if you lose, like, you're pretty screwed for the rest of the time.

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:21.900
Like, I feel like I had to lose quite a few times before I was like, okay, well, now it's over.

00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:23.560
But then that's me losing the game.

00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:32.920
I just want you to know I feel good about this conversation and that there's – you know, not only is there no way to prove it, but it's almost certainly in your favor.

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:34.620
The odds are in your favor.

00:44:34.760 --> 00:44:38.400
I guess I'm just saying that, like, I prefer to play –

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:40.300
As a god. A game like this.

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:45.540
Yeah, a game like this where, like, where I'm in so deep, right?

00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:47.120
Like, it's all or nothing.

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:51.260
But it's also not all or nothing if I can just go back and do it better the next time.

00:44:51.640 --> 00:44:54.920
I prefer to just start over than to live with my fans.

00:44:55.260 --> 00:44:56.460
Yeah. Okay.

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:57.540
I'm going to step in here.

00:44:57.800 --> 00:44:59.780
Especially in this age of save states and stuff.

00:44:59.840 --> 00:45:00.800
I mean, I do that too.

00:45:00.960 --> 00:45:06.240
But, like, I just feel like we – you know, I just don't think it was necessarily a fault of the games.

00:45:06.540 --> 00:45:07.420
Although it could have been.

00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:14.580
I will just step in here real quick just to remind everyone that I only agree with Mike if this happens early.

00:45:15.220 --> 00:45:16.060
I think mid-game –

00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:18.460
I also agree if it happens early.

00:45:18.820 --> 00:45:21.040
Mid-game, you can come back from losses.

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:23.480
Sure. A hundred percent.

00:45:23.720 --> 00:45:28.160
Also because, yeah, you'll be gaining a lot more gold, so you'll be able to rebuild your army and stuff like that.

00:45:28.280 --> 00:45:29.500
A hundred percent. I agree.

00:45:35.200 --> 00:45:37.640
I think we're all back on the same page here.

00:45:37.840 --> 00:45:46.720
So since we talked about raiding the castles, can we talk about the other minigame-like aspect, which is the tournaments, the jousting and everything like that?

00:45:46.780 --> 00:45:50.980
We briefly touched upon this, but I'd really just love to hear if there's any additional thoughts.

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:59.300
And also just the mechanic of the jousting and I think what they call the melee, but really just swinging your mace around.

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:02.380
It's different than the swords play.

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:11.780
So the objective for jousting here, you have your lance and you have to be very precise with this thing.

00:46:11.940 --> 00:46:18.320
And I think it's kind of cool how they do it in this first-person perspective where you have your lance and you've got to move it while your horse is moving.

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:22.380
So it kind of fights against you a little bit and you see your enemy coming up.

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:27.340
Long story short, you want to line the lance up with like the center area of their shield.

00:46:27.340 --> 00:46:28.740
Not the exact center.

00:46:28.800 --> 00:46:32.620
It doesn't have to be perfect, but it has to be pretty good in the center of this shield.

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:35.900
And then that will cause them to be knocked off their horse.

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:38.940
Then you will go to the melee section with the maces.

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:42.300
And it's not something where you just say like, okay, he's close.

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:43.840
Attack, attack, attack.

00:46:44.120 --> 00:46:45.660
You have to deal damage.

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:51.060
You have to let the mace actually swing for a while, which I thought was a pretty interesting thing.

00:46:51.300 --> 00:46:58.740
They could have maybe explained that a little better in the sense of like a power meter or a swing meter or something to show you.

00:46:58.840 --> 00:47:01.060
It's built in. Yeah, it's just built in.

00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:03.040
You just count how many times you saw.

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:06.540
No, no, no. So like, okay, first of all, the jousting.

00:47:07.560 --> 00:47:11.840
I was okay at jousting after however many times I did this.

00:47:11.980 --> 00:47:28.200
I still don't really know what all the variables are that like make you be able to get your lance over to the shield.

00:47:28.200 --> 00:47:32.700
I will say that both of these minigames like definitely feel weighty.

00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:34.020
I think that's good.

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:38.640
Like I imagine trying to line up a lance is pretty difficult.

00:47:39.880 --> 00:47:49.300
But when it comes to the swing in the chain portion, like there's visual cues as to like when you're supposed to hit that attack button.

00:47:49.480 --> 00:47:52.360
And it is like on the backswing.

00:47:52.360 --> 00:48:07.120
Like as soon as you are at the farthest point in that backswing and you are aiming at the correct like lower body or upper body, wherever the shield isn't, then you will land a blow.

00:48:07.400 --> 00:48:09.100
And sometimes it feels a little finicky.

00:48:09.280 --> 00:48:10.420
It's like, oh, I definitely hit him.

00:48:10.500 --> 00:48:15.340
But that sort of like I understood that pretty well.

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:16.820
Yeah, that's amazing.

00:48:16.820 --> 00:48:18.220
You just blew my mind with that.

00:48:18.620 --> 00:48:20.120
I guess I was just getting lucky.

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:25.040
I really did think you had to like let it spin a couple of times.

00:48:25.320 --> 00:48:27.000
I mean, you kind of do. That went up momentum.

00:48:27.260 --> 00:48:29.360
But that went totally over my head as well.

00:48:29.540 --> 00:48:36.140
And I'll confess that the reason that I generally didn't do any tournaments was because I never really bothered to like learn how to.

00:48:36.280 --> 00:48:38.560
Every time I did one, I was like, I have no idea what's going on.

00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:39.880
And I would just lose.

00:48:40.080 --> 00:48:42.500
And I'd be like, okay, I'm just going to go back to the other part of the game.

00:48:42.700 --> 00:48:47.880
But Sean, it sounds like you liked this tournament minigame.

00:48:47.880 --> 00:48:52.320
I mean, I understood them more than I understood the rest of the game.

00:48:53.400 --> 00:48:58.300
But I wouldn't say that either of them is particularly engaging.

00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:00.160
Yeah, I agree with that.

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:07.160
I think that that's like a good way of describing like a few of the minigames, not even just in the tournament.

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:12.540
But like understandable, not necessarily super, super engaging.

00:49:12.540 --> 00:49:17.780
Yeah, I mean, even going back to the rating, like I wouldn't say that that sword play was anything to write home about.

00:49:18.140 --> 00:49:23.340
If anything, it was tedious to just continue to do that in order to gain some gold.

00:49:23.620 --> 00:49:26.660
They could have probably came up with some more creative ways to get some gold.

00:49:29.020 --> 00:49:32.780
The next thing I guess we could talk about is just the defensive siege.

00:49:32.920 --> 00:49:40.180
What you guys were referring to is the, you know, shooting with your crossbow at the enemies as they try to attack your particular castle.

00:49:40.640 --> 00:49:48.860
Again, this is kind of like a first person point of view where you see the soldiers of the enemy pop up and you have to shoot them with your crossbow.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:50:00.280
So, you know, I think it's definitely like interesting that they chose like a unique way to like for each scenario, right?

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:08.500
So if you're doing an offensive siege, it's not like you're the soldiers now and you got to like dodge the guy with the crossbow.

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:16.520
Like it is nice that they came up with something for your castle being attacked that isn't just like we rolled a random number and it decided that this was the result.

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:19.100
Now you go out and battle army to army.

00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:22.340
Like a land battle. It is cool that, right, it is cool that this was done.

00:50:23.660 --> 00:50:26.360
But I don't really have any like thoughts on it.

00:50:27.260 --> 00:50:32.100
Again, it does just feel like a minigame in the way of the actual progress.

00:50:32.540 --> 00:50:34.820
It's not particularly hard.

00:50:35.040 --> 00:50:37.560
It's not easy though either.

00:50:37.700 --> 00:50:39.140
Like you do have to try this.

00:50:39.140 --> 00:50:44.520
If this happens to you, like you have to learn the ropes and hope to do better the following times.

00:50:44.520 --> 00:50:47.840
I didn't quite get the timing right away.

00:50:48.800 --> 00:51:01.300
Yeah, I guess like what I liked about it in theory, not so much in practice, was that your how well you do in this minigame.

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:05.600
It's not like it's not like you're playing as just this one crossbowman.

00:51:05.600 --> 00:51:16.560
But like however well you do, like that's how much casualties you've avoided, which is better, I think, than like an all or nothing thing.

00:51:16.680 --> 00:51:19.140
Like, oh, you successfully repelled them and suffered no casualties.

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:21.200
They're like, oh, you just died and everybody's dead.

00:51:22.420 --> 00:51:28.020
I appreciated that it's sort of like a war of attrition when it's like this.

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:33.860
But if you're being rated or if you're being attacked, like you're just not having a good time.

00:51:34.020 --> 00:51:37.000
So I didn't really like it that much.

00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:39.700
But I liked the thought that went into it.

00:51:40.860 --> 00:51:47.680
I'd say that I would have considered this the best minigame.

00:51:47.820 --> 00:51:48.720
Maybe I still do.

00:51:48.940 --> 00:51:52.380
But like the most, I think it's very balanced.

00:51:52.880 --> 00:51:56.820
And I think it's the most fun of the minigames personally.

00:51:57.500 --> 00:52:01.200
And I think, you know, it's challenging.

00:52:01.740 --> 00:52:03.200
It's challenging but not impossible.

00:52:03.620 --> 00:52:10.240
But the flaw with it for me is that, so just to paint the picture a little bit, you know, you have your crossbow at the bottom of the screen.

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:14.280
And you have to move around and shoot at enemies that pop up on your castle wall.

00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:16.940
And there's like three different levels of your castle wall.

00:52:17.040 --> 00:52:18.220
Like some spots are higher than others.

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:22.740
And you have to like adjust the distance that you're going to fire.

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:25.100
Or like I guess it's like the angle that you're holding your crossbow.

00:52:25.300 --> 00:52:26.720
Like how high up you're holding it.

00:52:27.580 --> 00:52:29.600
And you do that just with a tap of the D-pad.

00:52:29.980 --> 00:52:35.360
But there's no real like clear indication of how high you're aiming.

00:52:35.960 --> 00:52:37.580
Until you lose several times.

00:52:37.820 --> 00:52:42.240
Yeah. So like I'll shoot the guy at the top and then I want to shoot the guy at the middle.

00:52:42.900 --> 00:52:45.440
But like if I want to shoot the guy at the bottom, easy.

00:52:45.560 --> 00:52:48.400
Like as I'm moving the crossbow, I'm just holding down and I know I'm at the bottom.

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:52.900
And the same thing with when I shoot the guy at the top holding up and I know I'm aiming at the highest.

00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:59.460
But like to shoot at the middle, it's just so – because you have to be using the right or left button on the D-pad to move.

00:52:59.660 --> 00:53:01.940
And then like tapping the down button the one time.

00:53:02.060 --> 00:53:05.960
And like I'm just never clear where I'm aiming until I shoot and miss.

00:53:05.960 --> 00:53:08.180
And then I'm like, okay, I wasn't aiming at the right spot.

00:53:08.280 --> 00:53:22.900
Instead of like just this very – like there isn't a lot of information that comes from like where your character is until you have played it enough to realize that like, oh, if he's this many pixels up, that means he's going to fire at the guys on the ramparts.

00:53:22.900 --> 00:53:26.940
And if he's this many pixels down from that, like then it will be mid-range.

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:36.400
So I think that they could have fixed this just by like changing the sprite of your guy to actually like also change what looks like the angle at which he's firing.

00:53:36.640 --> 00:53:37.900
That's all that needs to be done.

00:53:37.900 --> 00:53:44.340
But instead it's just like copy-pasted but like offset however many blocks.

00:53:44.560 --> 00:53:47.840
It's kind of hard to describe if you haven't seen the screen.

00:53:48.440 --> 00:53:52.880
Like what I imagine some of our listeners are just sort of scratching their heads right now.

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:56.960
But that's what I would have done.

00:53:57.780 --> 00:54:15.360
Yeah. I mean I think even ideally – I mean obviously this might be like difficult to do but like put some sort of crosshair or even if it's not going to be a crosshair like just something in your lower right that just like says like high, mid, low or something.

00:54:15.360 --> 00:54:23.680
You can just see where you're aiming or just like has a little bar where you're just – it's like the section you're aiming lights up or something like that.

00:54:24.780 --> 00:54:27.400
Like just so you could – it's all happening so fast.

00:54:27.520 --> 00:54:32.240
You don't have time to like think about like wait, which way does it look like he's maybe aiming?

00:54:32.380 --> 00:54:34.720
Like because then they shoot at you pretty quickly.

00:54:34.720 --> 00:54:44.400
And then the other type of siege is where you're the attacker and to do this you must own at least one catapult.

00:54:44.860 --> 00:54:48.760
It has to be on your moving army with you.

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:49.940
On your person.

00:54:50.160 --> 00:54:51.760
Please have a catapult.

00:54:51.760 --> 00:55:01.360
And then when you choose this offensive siege, now you're greeted with what I would argue is the best minigame.

00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:03.580
I know, Joe, you like the crossbow one.

00:55:03.820 --> 00:55:05.060
I thought this was kind of fun.

00:55:05.380 --> 00:55:12.920
You're given a selection of like rocks, diseases, and like – what's the other thing?

00:55:12.940 --> 00:55:16.560
Greek fire. Yeah, like a – right, like a grenade, yeah.

00:55:16.980 --> 00:55:18.700
But 1100's grenade.

00:55:21.860 --> 00:55:27.540
The idea that you have to then like put – let's just say the rocks since you have to start with them no matter what for this to work out well.

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:30.540
You load the rocks onto the catapult one at a time.

00:55:30.680 --> 00:55:32.520
You have a limited inventory.

00:55:32.680 --> 00:55:35.840
It doesn't matter how strong your army is or how many catapults you have.

00:55:35.900 --> 00:55:41.000
You only ever have X amount of turns and you can only ever use a certain amount of things.

00:55:41.000 --> 00:55:48.580
I just think that's funny though that like you could have a whole fleet of catapults and like you only remembered to bring that many rocks.

00:55:48.700 --> 00:55:49.820
Your one, right, yeah.

00:55:51.400 --> 00:56:02.700
And then you load these rocks up and you have to hold down the A button to kind of load the – or load the spring to push down the catapult and then release it.

00:56:02.980 --> 00:56:11.060
And with that it launches your rock into the air and then hopefully at a particular point of the castle wall so that it creates a little hole.

00:56:11.060 --> 00:56:17.100
And then you would go to the slightly lower part of the castle from there and aim for that.

00:56:17.240 --> 00:56:30.940
And you can do that a maximum of four times but you really only need to do it like probably twice but I would say three for good measure to make sure that you then throw over the disease and the fire stuff successfully.

00:56:30.940 --> 00:56:33.540
Because you can't just launch that stuff at the castle.

00:56:33.540 --> 00:56:38.600
It will just act like it hit a wall or didn't make it or went over it or whatever.

00:56:38.820 --> 00:56:42.180
You have to make this incision first on the castle.

00:56:42.680 --> 00:56:43.980
And I don't know.

00:56:44.140 --> 00:56:47.640
It was one of those things where you have to learn the timing.

00:56:47.880 --> 00:56:52.260
But once you figure out where it happens, this was something I could get pretty consistently.

00:56:52.540 --> 00:56:56.600
So I enjoyed going back and doing this once I had my catapult ready.

00:56:56.700 --> 00:56:58.120
I felt good going into it.

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:03.840
I didn't make too many mistakes once I figured out the positions of the catapult.

00:57:03.940 --> 00:57:11.100
Then it just became a game of making sure that how many diseases am I going to throw at them or fires am I going to start.

00:57:11.660 --> 00:57:12.960
So I like that.

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:16.220
That's the one thing though that kept – because I like this one a lot too.

00:57:16.400 --> 00:57:27.280
But the one thing that kept it from me liking it more than the defense minigame is that I felt that this one was the most fully learnable.

00:57:29.300 --> 00:57:31.820
The first couple times it was like trial and error and I was figuring it out.

00:57:31.940 --> 00:57:33.100
But then after a while it was like, okay.

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:35.640
Because it clicks when you pull the catapult down.

00:57:35.860 --> 00:57:36.680
So I would like, wait.

00:57:36.800 --> 00:57:38.840
I think I did like – I would count seven clicks.

00:57:39.000 --> 00:57:41.340
Let go. That's the perfect first shot every time.

00:57:41.580 --> 00:57:42.840
Every single time I would get that.

00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:45.180
And then it was like, okay, now I count five clicks or something.

00:57:45.200 --> 00:57:46.040
I don't remember what they were now.

00:57:46.120 --> 00:57:48.340
But like, okay, that's the perfect second shot.

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:49.740
Now I got a perfect hole there.

00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:52.060
Now I just throw disease, fire.

00:57:52.060 --> 00:57:55.220
I feel like it was just – I still enjoyed it.

00:57:55.300 --> 00:57:56.760
It was still a cool little aside.

00:57:56.860 --> 00:58:01.060
But after a while I was like, okay, I know exactly how to maximize this.

00:58:01.720 --> 00:58:13.060
And then it got less – it was like the opposite of what I was saying for the siege – or not the siege, but for the infiltrating the castle ones where it was like, yeah, I can learn it.

00:58:13.080 --> 00:58:14.940
But I could still fail.

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:22.460
This one I just felt like it was like after a while I was like, okay, you can't really fail if you just memorize like exactly when to launch things.

00:58:22.680 --> 00:58:38.060
So regardless of, you know, if you make the – all four hits on the rocks and throw some diseases or whatever, you still have to then do a land battle, which also can just happen in a regular conquest too.

00:58:38.060 --> 00:58:46.000
If you're just going up against somebody else who happens to be in the same territory and you're trying to claim that land, you'll get into a fight with them that way as well.

00:58:46.120 --> 00:58:49.400
So this isn't exclusive to an offensive siege.

00:58:49.400 --> 00:58:52.180
This is just a land battle that can happen at any particular time.

00:58:52.600 --> 00:58:58.940
It's whatever that particular faction has available to them.

00:58:58.980 --> 00:59:00.520
It's not just like their entire army.

00:59:00.640 --> 00:59:03.980
That would be crazy. It's whatever they have on them versus whatever you have on you.

00:59:04.220 --> 00:59:09.360
On their person. To me, yeah, this is strictly a numbers game kind of thing.

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:13.900
I mean we talked about that leadership stat changing how well they respond to your commands.

00:59:14.200 --> 00:59:17.120
But, I mean, maybe you need like some really high leadership.

00:59:17.500 --> 00:59:22.940
For me, this is something where like you're either winning or losing as soon as you see what the numbers are.

00:59:23.120 --> 00:59:32.460
I never saw a situation where I was at 30 and they were at 40 and it was going to matter enough that anything other than a retreat was going to help me come out of that alive.

00:59:32.940 --> 00:59:37.940
Yeah. You know, that said, you know, what your army is made of does matter.

00:59:37.940 --> 00:59:45.400
Like if you have, you know, like 60 soldiers and they have 40, that's great for the start.

00:59:45.560 --> 00:59:50.060
But if they then have like eight knights, they might be able to take you down once you get to those knights.

00:59:50.220 --> 00:59:51.120
Those guys are strong.

00:59:51.400 --> 00:59:56.060
So like knights are much more effective in much smaller numbers than soldiers would be.

00:59:56.060 --> 01:00:01.040
But then, you know, catapults also like we were talking about how you can only ever use one in the offensive siege.

01:00:01.100 --> 01:00:04.860
But if you do have multiple catapults on you, this is where it counts.

01:00:05.080 --> 01:00:12.040
Yeah. I mean, it very is kind of it's it's predetermined in one sense.

01:00:12.040 --> 01:00:26.460
But you can also use different strategies, which I which I guess depending on your leadership stat and against their leadership stat and however guys you have and how many guys they have.

01:00:26.540 --> 01:00:32.140
Like, I guess if you were to switch it up a bit, you could still win a pitched battle.

01:00:33.380 --> 01:00:40.040
But it just seems to happen so fast, like immediately from the screen, like your guys start ticking down.

01:00:40.220 --> 01:00:48.140
So I think like you have to know exactly what you need to do from the beginning in order to make that correct choice.

01:00:49.060 --> 01:00:49.920
Like otherwise it is.

01:00:50.060 --> 01:00:51.160
Yeah, just that numbers game.

01:00:51.160 --> 01:00:54.520
And there might be some RNG thrown in that I don't actually know.

01:00:55.500 --> 01:01:07.520
Yeah. But I think even if you do know exactly what you want to do, like I was like I was just lucky enough that like my strategy because I always started with was often to just hold my ground, which is which is the first.

01:01:08.760 --> 01:01:09.600
Yeah. The default.

01:01:10.140 --> 01:01:10.940
But yeah.

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:17.800
But if like if I ever needed to change something because I saw I was losing, like there was just no time.

01:01:17.980 --> 01:01:24.760
Like there's no time at all to do any like it almost seemed pointless to ever change anything because it's like, well, by the time I just scroll down and try and select.

01:01:24.760 --> 01:01:27.520
I probably lost 20 men or something.

01:01:28.480 --> 01:01:30.400
But I did like that.

01:01:30.440 --> 01:01:38.120
Like once I started to learn that like, you know, knights also seem to make your soldiers decrease much more slowly.

01:01:38.300 --> 01:01:39.860
Like I guess they're protecting them or whatever.

01:01:40.180 --> 01:01:44.640
But like there were times where I would have much fewer soldiers than someone else.

01:01:44.720 --> 01:01:48.220
But if I had more knights, their soldiers would rapidly deplete, which was nice.

01:01:48.220 --> 01:01:55.120
But I could never say for sure like how much of my input was mattering or how much it was just predetermined.

01:01:55.240 --> 01:01:57.820
I appreciate that the commands aren't automatic.

01:01:58.140 --> 01:02:00.520
I think you only have like four things that you can possibly do.

01:02:00.740 --> 01:02:03.600
That gave me like some kind of feeling of a general, right?

01:02:03.760 --> 01:02:05.460
Like it's it wouldn't be instantaneous.

01:02:05.580 --> 01:02:06.940
So I did kind of like that.

01:02:07.040 --> 01:02:13.000
Like if you say retreat, you still kind of watch your numbers dwindle for like another second or two before they actually go away.

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:17.140
Yeah. So you do have to think about what you want to do early on in the battle.

01:02:17.240 --> 01:02:19.240
And that's why I said like for me, it was a numbers game.

01:02:19.360 --> 01:02:26.660
If I didn't think I had a chance, I wasn't going to let my army dwindle down and then decide like, OK, now I have like eight guys left.

01:02:26.780 --> 01:02:28.240
I know I'm not winning. Yeah.

01:02:28.420 --> 01:02:31.940
I just wish that they gave you an option to be like, hey, the battle's about to start.

01:02:32.060 --> 01:02:33.160
What's your initial command?

01:02:33.580 --> 01:02:34.800
Because it just defaults.

01:02:35.080 --> 01:02:36.500
It just defaults at that one command.

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:38.760
Like, well, I don't want to give them this command, but you have to start there.

01:02:38.880 --> 01:02:41.860
And then you have to spend time in the battle to switch to your other command.

01:02:41.860 --> 01:02:43.500
I like that idea a lot.

01:02:43.820 --> 01:02:53.580
Yeah. I think you could also add some flavor to it if like if it was only the attacker that could choose how to proceed and the defender had to start at that default.

01:02:54.900 --> 01:02:56.680
Right. Yeah, that would make sense.

01:02:57.460 --> 01:03:11.280
Don't you think it's interesting, though, that they went with like this being the one thing really other than, you know, making your turn decisions, this being the one thing that you don't really have control over?

01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:17.260
Like everything else is like a minigame that you personally are involved in in one way or another.

01:03:17.260 --> 01:03:24.420
And then this is the one where they're like, no, here you can just call out from the battlefield what you'd like them to do.

01:03:24.500 --> 01:03:29.040
But even those things aren't necessarily like prescriptive of what's going to happen.

01:03:29.220 --> 01:03:31.500
There's just like suggestions to your army.

01:03:31.580 --> 01:03:37.280
Right. I mean, I'm sure they change results on a computational level, but not something that we can see visually.

01:03:37.720 --> 01:03:46.680
So I think it's interesting that they didn't decide like, oh, for the land battles, you'll be one of those guys out on the field like attacking other soldiers.

01:03:46.680 --> 01:03:56.460
That's just sort of one of the things that I appreciated Nobunaga's revenge for was that like a land battle is like a tactics game, even though it is a very simplified version of one.

01:03:56.820 --> 01:03:58.680
Yeah, I guess I hadn't really thought about that, but that's true.

01:03:58.840 --> 01:04:05.060
Like, yeah, this could easily be like a sword fight where every hit you get counts as like you destroying some of their numbers.

01:04:05.340 --> 01:04:09.920
And every hit they get, you know, kind of like they do it in that, well, in other games, in this game.

01:04:10.040 --> 01:04:14.440
And also, I mean, no, we're jumping way ahead here, but like in Pirates.

01:04:15.680 --> 01:04:17.240
Ah. Right. Sid Meier's Pirates.

01:04:17.420 --> 01:04:40.080
Sid Meier's Pirates, yeah. So that's one of the bigger ideas I wanted to get back to is, you know, the inclusion of these minigames at all, right?

01:04:40.220 --> 01:04:45.840
Like, I'm not talking about this Conquest thing anymore because, again, that feels more like your general, you being the general.

01:04:46.040 --> 01:04:52.980
Of your army. The minigames, they do add an interesting flavor to the game.

01:04:52.980 --> 01:05:02.680
But do you almost wish that those became simulated as well in a sense that mattered more to stats, other stats that they could have added maybe?

01:05:02.920 --> 01:05:05.960
Things that could have been more like dice rolls?

01:05:05.960 --> 01:05:18.460
Or do you think that the game should have went even further on the minigame side and made everything about this game into like little subsections of its own game that you have to learn those parts to succeed further?

01:05:18.460 --> 01:05:22.580
Like, should traveling have been a minigame?

01:05:22.920 --> 01:05:25.720
Should, you know, I know it sounds crazy, but you could have done it, right?

01:05:25.720 --> 01:05:26.680
It's not unfathomable.

01:05:26.680 --> 01:05:29.560
At this point, I think you're making more of an adventure game.

01:05:30.100 --> 01:05:33.160
If you're like in direct control of your guy at all times.

01:05:33.160 --> 01:05:45.500
I think a strategy game needs to have some more like it needs to be stat based and also like decision based.

01:05:45.700 --> 01:05:55.120
So if more things are being simulated that I guess I'm sort of arguing against both sides here.

01:05:55.120 --> 01:06:14.280
What I'm trying to say is that this game is kind of is kind of schizophrenic where I'm sorry, bipolar, where there are some things that are like purely direct input and some things that are purely stat based.

01:06:14.560 --> 01:06:16.960
And they're kind of going out in random directions.

01:06:17.380 --> 01:06:24.120
And so it doesn't really have that much of a of an identity in that regard.

01:06:24.120 --> 01:06:31.320
But I think like if you made it all like direct input, like now you walk to where you need to go.

01:06:31.880 --> 01:06:35.760
I think that that is that's more of like an adventure game thing.

01:06:36.540 --> 01:06:42.100
And I think that like some of the simulation and dice rolling stuff that would make it a bit more strategy.

01:06:42.120 --> 01:06:48.520
If you had more more decisions to make like on a like on a map.

01:06:49.720 --> 01:06:51.440
I'm I'm rambling again.

01:06:52.320 --> 01:06:54.940
You're not rambling because ultimately I get it.

01:06:55.060 --> 01:07:06.220
Like this is a conscious choice from the developers to break up the probably what, you know, their kid facing audience would think of as the more boring part of the game.

01:07:06.320 --> 01:07:08.040
The part where numbers are just displayed.

01:07:08.360 --> 01:07:10.180
I don't think their target audience was kids.

01:07:10.180 --> 01:07:19.560
But for the NES it works out nicely that there are these mini games that happen and they they kind of split you in a way that you describe as bipolar.

01:07:19.820 --> 01:07:20.960
And I don't think is like wrong.

01:07:20.960 --> 01:07:26.220
But it's also just a chance to try something different for a video game.

01:07:26.320 --> 01:07:29.320
Right. Is that we've already seen the version where this whole game is just numbers.

01:07:29.820 --> 01:07:31.980
And we didn't find that to be essential.

01:07:31.980 --> 01:07:34.840
So this is another way of of tackling that.

01:07:35.000 --> 01:07:37.760
I'm not saying that they should have removed all the mini games.

01:07:37.760 --> 01:07:42.720
I'm not saying that the whole game should have just been an adventure game where you try to conquer England that way.

01:07:42.860 --> 01:07:44.600
This was the choice of the developer.

01:07:44.760 --> 01:07:49.360
So ultimately we can only judge it based on that and and have opinions on it.

01:07:49.560 --> 01:07:50.460
You know. True. Yeah.

01:07:50.600 --> 01:07:57.240
I was kind of thinking something similar to where like I wonder if the developers you know added some of these like mini game things and stuff.

01:07:57.240 --> 01:08:07.240
And I haven't I haven't looked into the other versions of this game but like added these mini games and stuff as like a as like a remedy for for putting this kind of game on the NES.

01:08:07.240 --> 01:08:19.160
Like because we hadn't seen it really we hadn't seen it we haven't seen a game on the NES like really work and be appealing to like a large audience yet on the NES like because of just the limitations and stuff.

01:08:19.280 --> 01:08:28.540
So it feels like maybe they were trying to like OK what's like some some NES video gamey stuff we can put in here and still keep the core of our game and kind of find strike that strike up that balance.

01:08:28.760 --> 01:08:36.980
I'd be interested to see like what the I think this is actually a pretty good port in terms of like what kind of things are in it.

01:08:36.980 --> 01:08:41.180
Like this is definitely really all these mini games are definitely in like the Amiga version.

01:08:42.220 --> 01:08:52.540
Right. But this is to Sean's point this is considered one of the better versions because of the improvements it makes from the Amiga version actually like in terms of balancing and and stuff like that.

01:08:52.680 --> 01:08:59.160
So I thought that was interesting. You know presentationally though too like the game is the game is pretty beautiful at times.

01:08:59.380 --> 01:09:03.860
Right. You know like some of the graphics for some of the mini games might look a little funny.

01:09:03.860 --> 01:09:16.320
But if you're just looking at like some of the portraits I'm thinking about you know for that's for those side missions where you like run into Robin Hood or when you rescue the princess like those portraits are pretty breathtaking.

01:09:17.060 --> 01:09:21.620
The jousting like with the the bugles like that was pretty cool.

01:09:22.100 --> 01:09:23.860
These are like they're static images.

01:09:23.860 --> 01:09:29.120
So I get that they were able to do better because they're not trying to make like full cut scenes out of them.

01:09:29.480 --> 01:09:34.880
I was surprised to even check out the Amiga version and see that their graphics are even better on that one.

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:42.860
I was like wow these are really nice looking pixel art the kind of like like a coffee book version of you know like that kind of large format.

01:09:42.860 --> 01:09:57.360
And it's it's funny that you you say that about the NES version because while it is like it's it's competent like this game got kind of popular because of its like beautiful pixel art on the computer like the PC versions.

01:09:58.840 --> 01:10:03.540
Whereas it's that that portion of it is kind of downplayed in this because it's an NES.

01:10:03.960 --> 01:10:07.480
But you're right like on the NES it still does look pretty good.

01:10:12.740 --> 01:10:16.840
So I you know I just realized I thought we were done but we're not there.

01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:21.060
We didn't talk about those side events these these random things that can sometimes happen.

01:10:21.480 --> 01:10:32.880
You can get married. Yeah yeah you can get married by rescuing the princess and also like yeah it's nice because you get married and now you see the the blank picture next to you on your status screen now is filled with her.

01:10:33.080 --> 01:10:36.300
So it's you know it's implying that nice marriage that you guys have as a family.

01:10:36.300 --> 01:10:42.800
But I think you also now like have joint ownership with another lord right like joint gain their territory.

01:10:42.800 --> 01:10:45.300
Oh he's yeah they're their vassal the vassal stuff.

01:10:45.640 --> 01:10:46.880
Yeah they're vassal yeah.

01:10:47.040 --> 01:10:48.500
So that that's that's pretty cool.

01:10:49.100 --> 01:11:05.700
But some other side events that can happen are that the the Normans can can steal from you and your vassals can rebel and you can run into Robin Hood who will then join you in your conquest.

01:11:06.300 --> 01:11:20.680
I think to the point where if if you if you recruit Robin Hood he will is it is it a cut scene or like does it actually affect the stats of the actual like conquest battle on land.

01:11:20.680 --> 01:11:22.380
I think it's a cut scene.

01:11:22.740 --> 01:11:24.480
I think he slips their throat when they're sleeping.

01:11:24.480 --> 01:11:27.420
I only saw I didn't actually recruit him. Got it.

01:11:27.600 --> 01:11:29.060
Yeah I mean like that's pretty cool though.

01:11:29.320 --> 01:11:36.920
Like the the idea of these side quests in this game they're not really and they don't necessarily matter.

01:11:37.120 --> 01:11:38.440
It's cool if they happen or not.

01:11:38.640 --> 01:11:43.380
But the the Norman stealing and the vassals rebelling are you know those have bigger implications.

01:11:43.380 --> 01:12:00.260
Yeah. But that reminds me like so much about like just the intrigue of more complex strategy games where they're not just one offs where they're just like those are actually they're actually like mechanics.

01:12:01.020 --> 01:12:13.440
So at least they are it is sort of reflected here as like wild cards that like you can sort of tip the scales a bit or have the scales tipped against you to add a little bit of flavor to it.

01:12:13.780 --> 01:12:14.680
Yeah I like those things.

01:12:14.780 --> 01:12:16.760
I wish I had known about Robin Hood earlier.

01:12:17.000 --> 01:12:18.540
I learned about him kind of late.

01:12:18.740 --> 01:12:21.420
But I so I actually never was able to use them either.

01:12:21.560 --> 01:12:23.660
Right. I thought that he like lent you numbers.

01:12:23.780 --> 01:12:30.160
So you're saying that he just gives you like he just kind of it's like a free conquer.

01:12:31.360 --> 01:12:43.140
I think I'm maybe it's because there are three different ways you can use him and maybe one of them is more of a cut scene thing and the others are buffs.

01:12:43.380 --> 01:12:44.600
I don't I don't quite remember.

01:12:44.760 --> 01:12:45.860
OK. Yeah.

01:12:45.960 --> 01:12:51.640
I think regardless he limits your your the enemy's soldiers like he helps make that number small.

01:12:52.120 --> 01:12:53.000
Hmm. Gotcha.

